# {{.Name}}

A synchronous Go library with mutable current values and immutable snapshots,
written by `gotogether init -kind library {{.Name}}`. Its production code uses
only the standard library. Ordinary tests call the actual API and record the
returned snapshot values; no simulator or instrumentation in the library is
needed.

```sh
go test -short -v ./...
gotogether simulate -binding {{.Name}}.binding.yaml -graph
./run-demo.sh
MUTANT=aliasSnapshot ./run-demo.sh
```

The tests require Quint; the demo additionally requires `gotogether` and Python3
on PATH. Each demo creates a fresh `logs/run-*` directory with actual native
observations, exact validation results and a bundled manifest. It replays the
same captured history; replay is distinct from executing the library again.
The demo exits 0 when the expected semantic verdict occurs, 1 on a failed check,
and 2 for an unknown mutant or a missing prerequisite. Tool errors do not count
as detecting the planted bug.

| file | purpose |
|---|---|
| `{{.Name}}.go` | `New`, `Put`, `Snapshot` and `Snapshot.Get`; snapshots own copied values |
| `model/{{.Name}}.qnt` | a bounded map model; captured values remain unchanged by later puts |
| `{{.Name}}.binding.yaml` | compares each actual `Get` result with the captured model value |
| `library_test.go` | synchronous API calls, native recording and the expected mutant verdict |
| `testdata/mutants/aliasSnapshot/{{.Name}}.go` | a source contrast that returns the mutable current map as a snapshot |

| test | expected result |
|---|---|
| `TestConforms` | the first snapshot still returns 1 after a later put stores 2 |
| `TestMutants` / `TestMutant` | aliasing is caught as `Mismatch("read")`, rather than a setup or tool failure |
| `TestDemoEvidence` | when invoked by the demo, writes fresh observations and a portable manifest |

The model deliberately covers one key (`counter`), values 0–2 and two snapshot
labels. Labels are inputs chosen by the observing driver to select the real
snapshot objects, not invented return values. This is a bounded safety contract,
not a proof of arbitrary keys, concurrency or object lifetime behavior.
`snapshotCountBound` is a safety property; `hasSnapshot` is an initially false
helper/reachability predicate, so it is not asserted as an invariant. There is no
eventual-completion claim for this synchronous API.

Make it yours by defining the library contract in the model, then adapting the
observing driver to your actual API results. Extend the model domain and reach
checks deliberately. Keep observations independent of the model: do not replace
an API result with the value that would make validation pass. For additional
negative controls, remove `obs.value` from an actual `read` record: that must be
an evidence error (exit 2), not credit for catching a source defect.
